# CAPTURE OWNERSHIP, the SCALAR-RESOURCE cell: no collection, no slice, no
# element view. A closure captures a plain `String` local and the source is then
# rebound 64 times, each rebind freeing the buffer the previous one held.
#
# The sibling repros (`closure_capture_then_mutate_source_uaf`,
# `closure_capture_inside_body_uaf`) all reallocate a captured COLLECTION, which
# admits the reading "a stale collection handle after a realloc". This cell has
# no collection at all, so it pins the rule at the level it actually lives:
# a capture is an ownership boundary, and an environment that holds a bitwise
# alias of a still-live source reads freed memory the moment the source moves on.
#
# ⚠ THE REBINDS ARE LOAD-BEARING — 64, not one, and the payload is heap-FORCED
# through `mk` so the freed buffer is a real heap allocation rather than
# immortal `.rodata` (a string literal has `cap = 0` and `gorget_string_free`
# no-ops on it).
#
# D1: a by-value capture is an independent snapshot AS OF CREATION, so the
# closure keeps `helloworld` no matter what the source becomes afterwards.
#
# INTENDED: prints `helloworld`, rc 0, both backends, no use-after-free.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    String s = mk("hello", "world")
    Callable[String()] f = (): s
    int i = 0
    while i < 64:
        s = s + "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx"
        i = i + 1
    print(f())
